vid05-frame-grammar control video-frame-hash-none-without-unknown VID-05
vid05-frame-grammar control video-frame-pair-unknown VID-05

# STA-01 and STA-02 (plan 08-17, D-16, D-43 retarget 2, hazard H2): the snapshot and the state wave is held by named mechanisms that each fail for their intended reason elsewhere in the gate. Twenty-two lines over six gates. STA-01 (the container and its refusals): the three writer faults (a little-endian header, serialized pad words, image bytes embedded), the two reader faults (the trailer not compared, a claimed length trusted), the three restore faults (the profile, the image digest, an instance mutated before validation) and four judges that each corrupt one expected value of the container literal, the refusal matrix, the refusal-atomicity oracle or the fuzz invariant and require the runner to notice. STA-02 (restore and run equals the uninterrupted run at the named hard points): the writer fault that drops the STIC rows and the two judges of the comparison and the row sweep, and the seven controls of the state step that keep the hidden-state checklist and the schema literal honest (a missing or extra row, an unknown witness or test, an exemption without a reason, a stale schema literal, a drifted class).
sta01-serializer-faults fault SNAPSHOT_LITTLE_ENDIAN STA-01
sta01-serializer-faults fault SNAPSHOT_PAD_SERIALIZED STA-01
sta01-serializer-faults fault SNAPSHOT_IMAGE_BYTES_EMBEDDED STA-01
sta01-reader-faults fault SNAPSHOT_TRAILER_UNCHECKED STA-01
sta01-reader-faults fault SNAPSHOT_LENGTH_TRUSTED STA-01
sta01-restore-faults fault RESTORE_MUTATES_BEFORE_VALIDATE STA-01
sta01-restore-faults fault RESTORE_PROFILE_UNCHECKED STA-01
sta01-restore-faults fault RESTORE_IMAGE_DIGEST_UNCHECKED STA-01
sta01-judges mutant state.judge.header-literal-corrupt STA-01
sta01-judges mutant state.judge.matrix-expectation-corrupt STA-01
sta01-judges mutant state.judge.refusal-expectation-corrupt STA-01
sta01-judges mutant state.judge.fuzz-invariant-detects STA-01
sta02-hardpoint-mechanisms fault SNAPSHOT_STIC_ROWS_OMITTED STA-02
sta02-hardpoint-mechanisms mutant state.judge.cut-compare-detects-divergence STA-02
sta02-hardpoint-mechanisms mutant state.judge.row-sweep-detects-omission STA-02
sta02-checklist-controls control state-checklist-row-missing STA-02
sta02-checklist-controls control state-checklist-line-extra STA-02
sta02-checklist-controls control state-checklist-witness-unknown STA-02
sta02-checklist-controls control state-checklist-test-unknown STA-02
sta02-checklist-controls control state-checklist-exempt-no-reason STA-02
sta02-checklist-controls control state-schema-literal-stale STA-02
sta02-checklist-controls control state-class-source-drift STA-02

# Phase 8 (plan 08-19, D-25): PRF-01. The benchmark tool refuses to print a figure for a run that differs from its committed
# golden (digest-first), and the check that asserts the absence of any measurement in a refused row can fail. A benchmark figure is
# never a claim, so there is no claim row; both mechanisms are test ids of the suite bench.
prf01-digest-first mutant bench.mutant.refuse-mutated-golden PRF-01
prf01-digest-first mutant bench.judge.refuse-has-no-timing PRF-01

# Plan 09-04 (EV-02, R2): the Phase 1 requirements FND-01 to FND-07, registered against mechanisms
# the gate already runs. Each is a control, so tools/check/run-controls.sh requires the step to exit
# non-zero and to print its pinned token: a step that does not actually read the record it claims to
# check cannot refuse a doctored one. No new fault site, control, test id or gate step is added.
# FND-01: a profile record is read, not described; a required key absent from the record and a tier
# tag outside the vocabulary must each be refused.
profile-record control profile-missing-key FND-01
profile-record control profile-tier5-tag FND-01
# FND-02: the claim ledger is consulted, not quoted; a tier outside the evidence-tier vocabulary and
# a claim standing above its recorded tier must each be refused.
claim-ledger control claims-unknown-tier FND-02
claim-ledger control claims-overstated-standing FND-02
# FND-03: a discrepancy row carries its conflict-resolution link; removing it must be refused, so a
# log that is only prose passes and the control fails.
discrepancy-log control discrepancies-missing-c17 FND-03
# FND-04: a manifest is a record of identity; a changed recorded digest and a broken recipe-to-source
# digest chain each make it a decoration rather than a record, and both must be refused.
fixture-manifest control manifests-digest-mismatch FND-04
fixture-manifest control manifests-recipe-source-digest FND-04
# FND-05: the provenance rule is enforced by a scan, not by review prose, only if a planted copied
# constant in an in-scope file is refused.
provenance-rule control denylist-hit FND-05
# FND-06: the reference notes are project-owned only if every row carries its source citation; a row
# without one must be refused.
cp1610-reference-notes control cp1610-row-without-cite FND-06
# FND-07: a defined time-base vocabulary that is not enforced is a document; a bare `clock` where a
# qualified unit is required must be refused.
time-base-vocabulary control glossary-bare-clock FND-07

# Plan 09-04 (EV-02, R2): the Phase 2 CPU and Phase 3 MEM requirements, registered against mechanisms
# the gate already runs. A fault cannot pass vacuously because the gate mutates the implementation at
# a named site and the suite must observe the mutation, so a surviving mutation fails faults-verdict;
# a control because run-controls.sh requires the step to exit non-zero and print its pinned token.
# GEN_EXPECT_MUTATION and GEN_MAP_MUTATION are generator faults (first column of
# data/expected/faults/cpu.ids and memmap.ids), so they resolve as `fault`, not as a test id.
# CPU-01: ADD_OVERFLOW proves the ALU result is compared; GEN_EXPECT_MUTATION proves the generated
# expectation table is read at all, since an unread table makes every opcode case pass.
cpu-opcode-expectations fault ADD_OVERFLOW CPU-01
cpu-opcode-expectations fault GEN_EXPECT_MUTATION CPU-01
# CPU-02: an acceptance model that is not executed cannot observe a reordered interrupt entry
# sequence or an inverted BUSRQ/BUSAK priority.
cpu-interrupt-model fault INT_ENTRY_ORDER CPU-02
cpu-interrupt-model fault BUSRQ_PRIORITY CPU-02
# CPU-03: the double-byte-data flag surviving an SDBD immediate must be observed, so the SDBD
# behaviour is compared rather than asserted in prose.
cpu-sdbd fault SDBD_DBD_NOT_CLEARED CPU-03
# CPU-04: the vector suite must fail on a reversed stack growth direction, so stack direction is a
# measured fact.
cpu-stack-and-reset fault PUSH_DIRECTION CPU-04
# CPU-05: a stepper whose reported trace is not read cannot show a dropped cycle stamp on its bus
# accesses.
cpu-bus-trace fault TCI_STROBE_STAMP CPU-05
# MEM-01: the map is generated from the profile descriptors and the data is checked, not asserted;
# a mutated generator and a literal map file with no profile must each be refused.
memmap-profile-generated fault GEN_MAP_MUTATION MEM-01
memmap-profile-generated control memmap-literal-orphan MEM-01
# MEM-02: both mechanisms are width behaviour, so a width claim with no failing mutation is a claim
# about code shape.
memmap-width-masking fault MEM_ROM_NO_MASK MEM-02
memmap-width-masking fault MEM_RAM8_NO_TRUNC MEM-02
# MEM-03: typed loading is proven by the refusal of a load published before the segment is validated.
content-loading fault LOAD_TABLE_BEFORE_VALIDATE MEM-03
# MEM-04: the refusal order is the requirement, and only a mutation of the order can test it.
content-refusal-order fault CHECK_ORDER_MALFORMED_FIRST MEM-04
# MEM-05: the documented import format is enforced only if a container that lies about its declared
# total byte count is refused.
ivsc-import-format fault IVSC_TRUST_TOTAL_BYTES MEM-05
# Phase 10 (plan 10-06): EV-05's three controls. A claim may not cite a handoff artifact and an
# untagged look/listen word is refused, so the two claims controls and the one text control are the
# mechanisms that make the handoff-ledger requirement fail when it is violated. Both gate names are
# new to this registry and both ids resolve through data/expected/controls/claims.ids.
claims control claims-handoff-artifact EV-05
claims control claims-handoff-row EV-05
text control text-untagged-look-listen EV-05
# Phase 11 (plan 11-05): TOOL-01's four mechanisms under one new gate name, runner-record. The recorded
# run and the replay of its log are equal only if the log holds the events the run applied and they are
# applied at the ticks they were recorded at, so the equality is the sole evidence for the requirement and
# each of its two ways of being wrong has a named fault: a writer that drops the last event line and a
# replay that applies every event one tick late (both judged by faults-verdict, both must-failing
# runner.record.replay-equals-straight). The two judge self-controls are the comparison's own liveness:
# record-replay-differs passes only when two reports that differ are refused, record-replay-inert passes
# only when the no-log witness is rejected as vacuous. TOOL-01 takes no exclusion-ledger row: its
# mechanisms are real, and an exclusion row would be the registry declining to cover what it just built.
runner-record fault RECORD_DROPS_LAST_EVENT TOOL-01
runner-record fault REPLAY_APPLIES_LATE TOOL-01
runner-record mutant runner.judge.record-replay-differs TOOL-01
runner-record mutant runner.judge.record-replay-inert TOOL-01
# Phase 13 (plan 13-01): RTS-01's ledger mechanism under one new gate name, rights-ledger. A ledger
# row that loses its disposition, a cleared row whose source id does not resolve in the sources
# registry, a row the inventory does not list and a row the operator page does not name are each
# refused by name inside the manifests step (tools/check/rights-lib.cmake), and the one control
# directory plants all four in a single run. RTS-01 takes no exclusion-ledger row: the mechanism is
# real, and an exclusion row would be the registry declining to cover what it just built.
rights-ledger control rights-ledger RTS-01
# Phase 13 (plan 13-03): RTS-02's naming limb under one new gate name, rights-naming. A ledger row's
# refused spelling is refused inside core/src/, runner/, tools/ and the six pinned operator pages and
# nowhere else, and a pre-existing occurrence is suppressed only by an exact "<token> <path>" pair in
# data/expected/rights-pinned.ids (tools/check/rights-lib.cmake, called from the files step). The
# control observes all three directions in one run: a pinned occurrence suppressed, an unpinned one
# refused, and a pin that matches nothing refused as stale. RTS-02 takes no exclusion-ledger row: the
# mechanism is real, and an exclusion row would be the registry declining to cover what it just built.
rights-naming control rights-naming RTS-02
# Phase 13 (plan 13-03): EV-06's claim ceiling under one new gate name, claim-ceiling. D-50 is two
# conditions inside tools/check/kinds/claim.cmake: no tier but source-expectation may stand
# established, and an established claim rests only on fields tagged tier1-primary-doc. Two must-fail
# controls isolate the two conditions, and one legal positive keeps both satisfied while the step
# fails for an unrelated reason, so a rule that refused every established claim would fail it. The
# live ledger is asserted, never edited: CL-0002 rests on three tier1-primary-doc fields and
# claims=28 provisional=27 is the criterion that catches an edit made to satisfy a test.
claim-ceiling control claim-established-non-source EV-06
claim-ceiling control claim-established-without-tier1 EV-06
claim-ceiling control claim-established-positive EV-06
# Phase 13 (plan 13-04): EV-07's ten D-09-06 registrations under one new gate name,
# d09-06-registration. Each of the ten mechanisms that already had a liveness guard and a re-runnable
# stand-in but no registry row gains the row that says so, and the row's kind is the kind that
# actually proves it: six `control` rows resolve through data/expected/controls/*.ids (five
# directories that already existed, important-gates-complete-uncovered, important-gates-excluded-malformed,
# sources-src-token-whole, manifests-output-anchor and memmap-generator-refused, plus the new
# important-gates-excluded-stale directory this plan adds), and four `mutant` rows resolve through the
# new data/expected/runner-selftest.ids, which the runner-selftest step must actually run
# (runner-selftest-missing). This narrows EV-07's literal "mutant row" wording: a mutant row for a
# mechanism a control directory already proves would be a second registration of one proof, and
# inventing a selftest to re-prove it would add a case with no new evidence. Zero new faults holds:
# ten faults here would cost about 4.4 hosted cpu-s, 82 percent of the milestone's allowance, on the
# step already 29 percent of the gate. EV-07 takes no exclusion-ledger row: the mechanisms are real,
# and an exclusion row would be the registry declining to cover what it just built. The baseline is
# re-derived by running the step, never quoted: lines and gates are its own printed stats.
d09-06-registration control important-gates-excluded-stale EV-07
d09-06-registration control important-gates-complete-uncovered EV-07
d09-06-registration control important-gates-excluded-malformed EV-07
d09-06-registration mutant st-toy-drift EV-07
d09-06-registration mutant st-deviation-unused EV-07
d09-06-registration mutant st-pass-line EV-07
d09-06-registration control sources-src-token-whole EV-07
d09-06-registration control manifests-output-anchor EV-07
d09-06-registration control memmap-generator-refused EV-07
d09-06-registration mutant st-memmap-generator-missing EV-07
# Phase 14 (plan 14-04): TOOL-07, SCN-03 and CPU-06 under three new gate names, flatwrap, cart-role
# and cpu06. These five rows are the GUARDRAILS 2.4 debt plan 14-01 recorded by name and plan 14-03
# repeated: 2.4 wants each new check's must-fail row in the check's own commit, but this step refuses
# a row naming a Pending requirement, and all three requirements stay Pending until this commit. So
# the rows and the three flips land together, and the deviation closes here over exactly the five
# mechanisms 14-01's table names, no more and no fewer. Two of the phase's new ids owe no 2.4 row and
# are named here so a reader does not have to count: pack.judge.flatwrap-tokens is a coverage diff
# (its failure is a mismatch between the pin and the literals the source can print, which needs no
# scratch mutation, so it has no stand-in limb to register), and runner-process.cart-user.refuse-derived
# is a refusal asserted by exit status plus one typed line, not an equivalence with a stand-in. A row
# for either would register a mechanism that does not exist. Zero new faults holds: the one fault row
# below is a fault that already exists, registered because CPU-06's flip owes its coverage.
# flatwrap: TOOL-07's "build-only command, derived material never committed". The tool is build-only
# and its sources are the build's, so the control is the c-build unlisted-source guard, shown to fail
# when a tools/flatwrap/*.c file is not listed (the control's own probe file reproduces it).
flatwrap control c-build-unlisted-flatwrap-source TOOL-07
# flatwrap: the printed digest is the file's digest by an implementation other than the core's
# (cmake -E sha256sum), and one flipped input byte moves it, so the equality cannot pass vacuously.
flatwrap mutant pack.judge.flatwrap-digest-live TOOL-07
# cart-role: the report's slot.cart.sha256 equals the digest the wrap tool printed, and one flipped
# input byte moves both; the same-input-twice stand-in shows the equality is live.
cart-role mutant runner-process.judge.flatwrap-roundtrip SCN-03
# cart-role: the route's pass path is shown able to fail. The case requires the unmutated run to meet
# its pinned outcome AND a one-byte EXEC mutation to give exit 14 with IVS_E_DIGEST_MISMATCH, so
# neither half can pass alone. The id is judge-shaped for exactly that reason: a `mutant` row resolves
# only through `<suite>.judge.<slug>` or `<suite>.mutant.<slug>`, and a case id without the judge
# segment would be important-gates-unknown-id.
cart-role mutant runner-process.judge.owner-run-cart-pass SCN-03
# cpu06: the profile field's effect is shown able to break. The fault's must-pass is
# cpu.UNIT-INT.accept-keeps-intff, so the not-cleared setting is the half that must keep passing: the
# both-settings pin and its must-fail mechanism in one row. The fault already exists (no new fault).
cpu06 fault ACCEPT_CLEARS_INTFF_IGNORED CPU-06
# host (plan 15-03, TOOL-08): the digest equality's two must-fail limbs, each a committed
# runner-process judge self-control rather than a new fault. A stub that prints the golden's nine
# lines with no frame is refused for `frames-zero`, so the "frames>0" limb is load-bearing and the
# equality cannot pass on a frontend that rendered nothing.
host mutant runner-process.judge.host-vacuous TOOL-08
# host: the pass golden's lines with the poll dropped (the empty-chain `digest.input` the empty-poll
# run observed) are refused for `digest-diff`, so the equality is proved live against a second,
# measured input state rather than against a constant. Both ids are judge-shaped because a `mutant`
# row resolves only through `<suite>.judge.<slug>` or `<suite>.mutant.<slug>`; a plain
# `runner-process.host.*` id would make this row important-gates-unknown-id.
host mutant runner-process.judge.host-digest-diff TOOL-08
# host (plan 15-04, TOOL-08, review CR-01): the bounded input schedule refuses rather than truncates. The
# mechanism is the committed harness mode `--schedule-full`, a host with 5000 events pending in one drain:
# it must be refused with nothing delivered and with the poll stopped short of 5000, so the refusal is the
# schedule's bound and not a host that ran out. Its witness pair is `--schedule-long` (8000 events over two
# calls, 4000 at a time, every one of them polled and both calls succeeding), which a schedule that never
# reclaimed its applied prefix fails - the silent loss the review measured. The id is judge-shaped because
# a `mutant` row resolves only through `<suite>.judge.<slug>` or `<suite>.mutant.<slug>`.
host mutant runner-process.judge.host-schedule TOOL-08
# host (plan 15-04, TOOL-08, review WR-01): a refused load leaves nothing committed. The mechanism is the
# committed harness mode `--refused-load`, a four-byte cartridge the core refuses: the instance digest must
# be unmoved, the run refused, and the same call with the right images must then succeed and move the
# digest. The witness pair is what a bare `load.refused 1` could not establish: the refusal cannot pass as
# a machine that is simply dead, and a half-loaded machine cannot answer a digest.
host mutant runner-process.judge.host-load-refused TOOL-08
# host (plan 15-04, TOOL-08, review WR-02): the judge self-control's own guard. `self_control` accepted a
# stand-in that failed for its intended token AND others, so a stand-in that differs from a passing output
# in two limbs - the pass golden's lines with `frames 13`, which an empty-poll judge finds identical to the
# pass output (empty-poll-identical) and still carrying the pass run's input (empty-poll-idle) - must be
# refused as judge-wrong-reason. Without this row the two digest limbs above would be guarded by a control
# that cannot tell a one-limb stand-in from a two-limb one.
host mutant runner-process.judge.host-two-limb TOOL-08
# release-posture (plan 16-04, PKG-06): the three mechanisms that hold the release-decision line
# ("the release ships the static package and the source archive; no shared object, no host shim, no
# content"). Every row below names a mechanism the phase *registered* rather than created: the two
# controls were built in plan 15-02 and the selftest in plan 15-02, so faults, steps and controls do
# not move here. The surface each row proves is named in its comment, because the tree, an archive and
# an installed prefix are three different surfaces and no one of them covers another.
# release-posture: a shared object is refused by name in the tree. The control plants a tracked .so in
# a copy of the toy tree and requires the refusal; plan 16-04's own probe proved the same rule on a
# committed path in the real tree (one finding for probe.so, none for probe.txt), which is the limb a
# control cannot reach. That probe is a throwaway check and is deliberately not a row here.
release-posture control files-unlisted-binary-so PKG-06
# release-posture: the same rule for the second extension family, so the refusal is the list and not
# one spelling - a rule that knew only `so` would let a `.dylib` through on the same surface.
release-posture control files-unlisted-binary-dylib PKG-06
# release-posture: the object cannot enter the installed package. The selftest plants
# lib/libivs_retro.so in a copy of the installed prefix and requires pkg-inventory-extra, so a future
# install rule on the build-only host target fails there first; the eleven-path inventory
# (data/expected/pkg-install-files.ids) is what makes the absence a check, and `prefix-files=11` is
# that same statement on every gate line. The release-assets case `so-member` (a synthetic archive
# whose own list names the object) is not registered: a `mutant` row resolves only through the three
# selftest inventories or a `<suite>.judge.<slug>`/`<suite>.mutant.<slug>` test id, and a name outside
# those inventories would make this row important-gates-unknown-id.
release-posture mutant host-so-absent PKG-06
# cart-role (plan 17-02, EV-08, criterion 3): the flatwrap-to-shim join. The harness's digest.images for the
# container the wrap tool wrote equals the runner's digest.images for it, and the runner's slot.cart.sha256
# equals the digest the tool printed; one flipped input byte must move both, and the committed stand-in that
# replays the FIRST container's lines against the SECOND container is refused with join-images. The
# same-input-twice stand-in is therefore the case's own second container, judged by the same shim_join, so the
# must-fail demonstration is repeated on every gate run instead of once. The gate name is the existing
# cart-role: no new gate name is minted, and no new fault, step or golden is produced.
cart-role mutant runner-process.judge.flatwrap-shim EV-08
# count-gate (plan 17-03, EV-08, lesson 32 clause 1): a count gate names the file it counts, and the count is
# taken from that file. The pkg-install step's check_inventory reads data/expected/pkg-install-files.ids (eleven
# paths) and prints that file's own name as pkg-inventory-list when it is missing, so a count read from anywhere
# else is a finding rather than a number; the committed `extra-file` case copies the installed prefix, plants
# share/ivs/stray.txt in the copy, and requires `pkg-inventory-extra: share/ivs/stray.txt` from the same
# check_inventory the real prefix is judged by. The stand-in is committed code inside the step, so the
# demonstration is re-run on every gate run and reported as the step's own pkg-selftest=20/20 token, instead of
# being built and deleted once. The row therefore POINTS at a mechanism that already exists - the same case is
# registered for PKG-01 under the step's own gate name - rather than minting a case to register: a new case
# would cost 0.05 hosted cpu-s and reprice this phase from 0.11 to 0.16, and the phase's priced line stays 0.11
# (one process id plus the two 0.005 rows). The gate is named `count-gate`, not `pkg-install`, because the row
# registers a CONTRACT rather than a step, exactly as d09-06-registration registers one; the new gate name is
# deliberate and is what makes the gate line's `gates` token read 120. Lesson 32's clause 3 (no verdict decided
# by a pipeline tail) is already registered as d09-06-registration mutant st-pass-line EV-07 and is cited there,
# not duplicated: a second row for one proof is the second registration plan 13-04 named and refused. Clause 2
# (a baseline names the plan's own start commit sha) has NO mechanism - no gate step parses plans - and is
# therefore not registered here: a row for it would register a mechanism that does not exist.
count-gate mutant extra-file EV-08

# Phase 18 (plan 18-02, TOOL-02 TOOL-09): the inspection surface's four committed stand-ins and the one
# control for the artifact class. Five rows, two gate names - four `mutant` rows under the EXISTING
# `runner-process` gate name, whose stand-ins are cases inside the `runner-process` suite that the gate
# re-runs on every run, and one `control` row under the new gate name `files`, which is the step that
# enforces the extension refusal. Every mechanism is a new id in an existing suite, a control directory
# or an in-step selftest: zero new faults, zero new steps, zero new goldens.
#
# The four trace stand-ins each fail for its own stated reason and each is judged by the SAME function
# the real case uses (GUARDRAILS-v0.4 2.7, 2.8): trace-empty fails `records > 0`; trace-repeat-tick
# fails the fifth equality's `not all equal` clause - a constant column IS non-decreasing, so a
# monotonicity-only check would accept it, which is what makes this stand-in the guard's own negative
# control; trace-pokes-register replaces one `digest.*` line with another domain's value, is refused by
# the suite's digest comparison, and is then fed to the tool's own reconciliation, which must refuse it
# with `inspect-reconcile`, exit 1 and no output file; break-always-hits sets the non-hit count to 1 and
# is refused by the hit/non-hit pair. The stand-ins and their guards landed in plan 18-02's Task 2
# commit and these rows land in Task 3's, because this step derives its accepted set from the
# traceability table and refuses a row naming a Pending requirement - the derived-accepted-set rule
# (GUARDRAILS-v0.4 2.14) is what forces that split, and the plan records it as a deviation.
runner-process mutant runner-process.judge.trace-empty TOOL-02
runner-process mutant runner-process.judge.trace-repeat-tick TOOL-02
runner-process mutant runner-process.judge.trace-pokes-register TOOL-02
runner-process mutant runner-process.judge.break-always-hits TOOL-02
# The control row is TOOL-09's own refusal: a tracked `ivst` in the tree must be refused by name, and
# `files` is the step that owns `tools/check/binary-ext.txt`. The row is the phase's one new gate name,
# which is why the gate line's `gates` token reads 121 rather than 120.
files control files-unlisted-binary-ivst TOOL-09
